Both describe what an object must provide. An `ABC` uses nominal typing: a class must inherit from it. A `Protocol` uses structural typing: any class with the right methods matches, with no inheritance needed.

```python
from typing import Protocol


class Greeter(Protocol):
    def greet(self) -> str: ...


class English:                      # does not inherit Greeter
    def greet(self) -> str:
        return "hello"


def welcome(g: Greeter) -> str:
    return g.greet()


print(welcome(English()))  # hello
```

Type checkers accept `English` here. Use an ABC when you want shared code or runtime enforcement.

