An independent set is a group of vertices with no edges between any two of them. Finding the largest one is NP-hard on general graphs, but it is solvable in polynomial time on trees and bipartite graphs. On a tree, a simple greedy rule works: take the leaves.

```python
def max_independent_tree(adj, root=0):
    chosen = set()

    def dfs(v, parent):
        for w in adj[v]:
            if w != parent:
                dfs(w, v)
        if not (set(adj[v]) & chosen):
            chosen.add(v)

    dfs(root, -1)
    return chosen


adj = {0: [1, 2], 1: [0, 3], 2: [0], 3: [1]}
print(sorted(max_independent_tree(adj)))  # [2, 3]
```

Post-order selection takes a vertex only if none of its neighbors is already chosen.

